Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

selFromPolicy sends a non-zero XfrmPolicy.SrcPortMask or DstPortMask as
given, in network byte order. Upstream derives each selector mask from its
port alone, all ones for a non-zero port and zero for port 0, so neither an
exact port 0 nor a mask-aligned block can be asked for. The kernel matches a
selector port as (flow ^ sel) & mask (__xfrm4_selector_match,
net/xfrm/xfrm_policy.c) and reads an ICMP type into the source port and its
code into the destination port (xfrm_flowi_sport, xfrm_flowi_dport,
include/net/xfrm.h), so the
upstream rule cannot express ICMP type 0 (Echo Reply) exactly, nor a code
range as the aligned blocks that cover it. RFC 4301 Section 4.4.1.1: "This
16-bit selector can contain a single type and a range of codes, a single type
and ANY code, and ANY type and ANY code." A zero mask keeps the upstream
derivation, so every caller that sets no mask is unchanged. XfrmPolicy gains
DstPortMask and SrcPortMask, which the policy reader and the reader of a
state's nested selector fill from the kernel.

Its tests, TestXfrmSelectorPortMaskWrite and TestXfrmSelectorPortMaskRead in
xfrm_selector_port_mask_linux_test.go, check the selector bytes the writer
produces and the masks the policy and state readers report.

No upstream pull request is opened yet for this change.

Needs netlink-xfrm-04-readback-message-family.patch, and so
netlink-xfrm-01-state-readback-esn-replay.patch: its state hunk realigns
the nested selector literal that 04 rewrites.

Applies unchanged to upstream main (e0e26cd, 2026-11-13) after the patches it
needs, not on its own, and its test passes there unprivileged.

Delete this patch when go.mod requires an upstream release that contains the
change.

